LANE BUDGET — proposal patch for the agent-team chain's own reviewer lane.
Lead-owned files: a seat may ONLY read and cite them, never edit them (chain law
11), so this file is the handover and the fix travels as hunks.

TARGET: .github/scripts/turn_segment.sh   (the duty-reviewer dispatch, the
        quality-assurance-engineer arm)
STATUS: PROPOSAL — NOT APPLIED. The target is Lead-owned.
APPLY:  git apply --check docs/lane-budget-fix.patch
        git apply       docs/lane-budget-fix.patch
ROT-PROOF: python3 scripts/chain_patch_check.py grades THAT this patch still
        applies to the carrier the chain runs, and
        python3 scripts/lane_budget_check.py grades WHY: its `green` control is
        this exact pair of repairs applied to a copy, and the copy answers 0.
WHAT IS BROKEN — measured in the lane, not reasoned from the prose:

  The duty reviewer was rotated (Owner order 2026-10-25, QA-3 hire) to
  groq/qwen3.8-27b@goose as the PRIMARY call of the review arm. goose sends the
  repo's own AGENTS.md inside EVERY request, and that single file is 68878 bytes
  = 22895 tokens against Groq's free-tier input budget of 7000 tokens per
  minute. Probed in this container with the shipped env recipe (goose 1.56.0,
  OPENAI_HOST=https://api.groq.com, GOOSE_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.8-27b, a 30-byte
  prompt):

    /tmp          (no AGENTS.md)        ANSWERED, rc 0, request under the cap
    /tmp/ctx-ab   (AGENTS.md only)      rejected, Requested 22895  req_01m5the92kehhahwngsv266z53
    /workspace    (the real lane)       rejected, Requested 22892  req_01m5thd10ream8chgszete560c

  So the carrier is AGENTS.md alone — 3.0084 bytes per token — and the review
  arm's first call can never be served here. The file this beat actually left
  behind says the same thing at a bigger number (Requested 44003, after tool
  output piled on), then goose tried to compact and was refused again: Limit
  1000, Requested 1932 on OUTPUT tokens. Both halves of the free tier are below
  what one goose session needs, so this is not load, not a transient, and not
  fixable by retrying: the rotation as written could only ever produce
  review-missing, and a branch with no verdict cannot land.

WHY THE FALLBACK DID NOT SAVE IT — the second hunk:

  A first-request refusal exits non-zero (measured rc 1), so the fallback does
  run in that shape. But a session that dies MID-flight leaves its own error
  text as the whole answer, and the chain reads that file for `VERDICT:` only —
  nothing distinguishes "the reviewer reviewed and forgot the token" (the
  0659 class, already a harness) from "the provider never answered". The
  guard added here reads the markers the PROVIDER prints, so a dead lane is
  recorded as a dead lane.

WHAT THE HUNK DOES:

  1. puts a lane that can answer FIRST (deepseek-v4.1-flash@qoder, the same
     brain the turn seat proves every beat) and keeps groq/qwen3.8-27b@goose as
     the fallback — QA_MEMBER is NOT touched, so the reviewer-id roster
     scripts/review_verdict_check.py compares across the two carriers still
     agrees, and no verdict is re-attributed;
  2. greps the review file for the provider rejection markers right after the
     dispatch, in either outcome.

OUT OF THIS PATCH'S DOMAIN (so the next reader does not expect it here): the
provenance stamping a fallback swap still cannot see — which member ACTUALLY
answered — is docs/review-provenance-fix.patch, and it does not overlap these
hunks. If both are landed, apply review-provenance-fix.patch FIRST (its hunks
sit further down the same file) and re-run both checks.

